**What the reviewer gets.** The flag dialog in the review queue now carries a third decision block, **Delegate details**, sitting with the room and payment-override blocks. It shows the name and gender currently on the record — `Member Guest0 Mensah · male` — and offers **Correct name / gender** for a super admin, opening the same pre-filled correction form as the desk and the bookings list, with its required reason. The queue rows only carried the joined `guestName` label, so [agcCheckin.ts](convex/agcCheckin.ts#L997) now returns `title`, `firstName` and `lastName` as well, the same fields I added to the desk sheet in the previous change.

Two details worth naming because they're what makes it usable next to the other actions. A correction is deliberately **not** the review decision: the flag stays open with the desk's note until the reviewer clears it, so fixing the record doesn't silently close the question the desk raised. And because the corrector and the room picker share the row, the corrected gender is what the bed filter uses on the very next action — flag a delegate who turns out to be female and the next room offered is a women's one, not the men's room their old record would have matched. I also handled the flag whose delegate has gone from the file: the block says so and offers no form, rather than opening an empty one.

**The gate is unchanged.** Correcting a delegate is still a super admin decision, and I did not widen it: the accommodation admin — who owns this queue — gets a line telling them to raise it with a super admin, instead of a button that would fail at the server. If you want the Accommodation Admin to be able to make the correction themselves, that's a deliberate authorization change and I'd rather you ask for it explicitly.

