Pushed as **8259a15** (`2454bd9..8259a15`, clean tree).

**What's on the check-in page now.** A strip of eight stat tiles sits at the very top of the arrival desk, under the alert banners and above the search — same small-tile design as the room assignment screen (label, big number, one-line hint), so the two screens read alike:

- **Checked in** — with how many delegates are assigned
- **Checked out** — noting that beds stay held
- **Not arrived** — amber while anyone is still expected
- **Keys issued** — out with delegates
- **Keys returned** — back at the desk
- **Unassigned** — rose when anyone still has no bed
- **Reserved rooms** — with the bed count held back
- **Faulty rooms** — out of inventory, rose when any

The counts follow the desk, not the whole site: with a building selected (or a check-in account posted to one), reserved and faulty rooms are that building's only, matching the sheet beneath. While the overview loads, the strip shows eight skeleton tiles rather than empty boxes.

**Backend.** The arrival overview only counted keys out; it now also returns `keysReturned`, so the strip shows both directions of the key ledger instead of deriving one client-side. Everything else was already in the overview payload.

The drawer's detailed overview (payment overrides, review flags, sync exceptions, per-type occupancy) is untouched — the strip is the at-a-glance row for the counter, the drawer remains the deep read. The help runbook's desk section now describes the strip and notes that a posted check-in account opens on its own building.

**Checks:** `npx tsc --noEmit` ✓, `npx eslint` on the changed files ✓, `npx vitest run` **255/255** across 33 files ✓ (the existing check-in test now also asserts the key counters read 1 out / 1 returned after a check-out that returns a key), `npm run build` ✓. No browser pass — the console needs staff credentials, so the strip's layout is verified by types, lint and build rather than by eye.
