Pushed as `a252b87`.

## Delete action

**Server** — `tourOrders.remove` in [tourOrders.ts](convex/tourOrders.ts) does three things atomically in one mutation: deletes the receipt blob (guarded so an already-expired file doesn't block the delete), deletes the order row, and writes an audit entry before the row disappears, capturing the reference, email, total, currency, payment status, line items and whether a receipt was attached. That last part matters: once the row is gone the audit log is the only place those facts survive.

**Access** — restricted to `["admin"]`. Registration and finance staff can still change payment status as before, but can't erase the record, and the UI hides the button for them rather than letting them hit a server error.

**UI** — in [Admin → Tours → Orders](src/app/admin/(console)/tours/page.tsx), opening an order shows **Delete order** next to the status buttons. It asks for confirmation naming the reference and guest, warns that the receipt goes with it and that it can't be undone, then closes the sheet on success. The delete button is only rendered for the `admin` role.

## Checks

`tsc` and eslint clean, `npm run build` passes, and the suite is now **36 files / 275 tests**, with two new cases in [tourReceipts.test.ts](tests/tourReceipts.test.ts): an admin delete removes the row *and* the receipt blob while leaving a `tour_order.delete` audit entry containing the reference and `hadReceipt`, and registration staff get a rejection with the row still intact. Functions are pushed to your dev deployment.

One thing I deliberately did not do: the test booking I created last turn (`HC44621`, `status-check@example.com`) is still there. Deleting it needs an admin session, and minting a session for one of your admin accounts to do it isn't something I'd do unprompted. It's now two clicks: Admin → Tours → Orders → open it → **Delete order**. If you'd rather I clear it directly, give me a dev admin login or just say so and I'll remove it through the UI in the shared browser once you're signed in.
